
     <worksheet xml:id="moment-generating-function">
         <title>The Moment Generating Function | Special Mathematical Expectations (2.3) Part 2 </title>
     
     <page>
    <definition xml:id="def-moment-generating-function">
    <title>Moment Generating Function (B:2.3-1)</title>   
        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>X</m> be a discrete R.V.
      with space <m>S</m> and pmf <m>f(x)</m>. If there exists a positive number
      <m>h</m> such that
      <md>
        E(e^{tX}) = \sum_{x\in S}e^{tx}f(x)
      </md>
      exists and is finite for <m>-h \lt t \lt h</m> (interval around zero),
      then <m>M(t) = E(e^{tX})</m> is called the
      <alert>moment generating function of <m>X</m> (mgf)</alert>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>
  

    <p>
      Suppose your sample space is <m>\{x_{1}, x_{2}, \dots, x_{k}\}</m>. Then
      <md>
        M(t) = e^{tx_1}f(x_{1}) + e^{tx_2}f(x_{2}) + ... + e^{tx_k}f(x_{k}).
      </md>
      Therefore the coefficient of <m>e^{tx_i}</m> is <fillin/>.
    </p>
<exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <alert>Example 1</alert>: Suppose <m>X</m> is a RV with the following
      moment generating function. Find the pmf.
      <md>
        M(t) = \frac{1}{2}+ \frac{1}{8}e^{t} + \frac{1}{4}e^{2t}+ \frac{1}{8}e^{4t}
      </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
  <exercise workspace="4in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            <alert>Example 2</alert>: Suppose <m>X</m> is a RV with the following
      moment generating function. Find the pmf.
      <md>
        M(t) = \frac{0.3e^{t}}{1-0.7e^{t}}.
      </md>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</page>
<page>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
             <alert>Neat little trick with Moment Generating Functions</alert>: Using
      the theory of Laplace Transforms, it is known that if <m>M(t)</m> exists
      for <m>-h \lt t \lt h,</m> then the <m>k^{th}</m> derivative of
      <m>M(t),</m> <m>M^{(k)}(t)</m> exists when <m>t=0.</m> Let's look at two
      of these derivatives.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                  <m>M'(t) =</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    
     <task workspace="2.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                  <m>M''(t) =</m>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
     <task workspace="1in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                  Summary:
