        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise workspace="3.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Back to Example 1 where <m>X</m> had the mgf
      below. Let's use the moment generating function to find
      <m>\mu, \sigma^{2}</m>:
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>{M(t) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{8}e^t + \frac{1}{4}e^{2t}+ \frac{1}{8}e^{4t}}</m>
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
</page>

<page>
<exercise workspace="3.5in">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Roll a fair die. Let <m>X</m> be the number of trials needed until we see
      the first six. Let us denote by <m>S</m> a <q>success</q> (rolling a <m>6</m>
      ) and <m>F</m> be a <q>failure</q> (rolling anything other than a <m>6</m> ).
    </p>

    <p>
      What is the space of <m>X</m>? What is the pmf? What is the mgf?
        </p>
    </statement>
</exercise>
<definition xml:id="def-geometric">
    <statement>
        <p>
            Suppose the pmf of a random variable <m>X</m> is
    </p>

    <p>
      <m>f(x)=p\cdot q^{x-1},\,\, x=1,2,...</m> where <m>q=1-p</m>
        </p>
        <p>
            Then <m>X</m> is said to have a <fillin characters="25"/> distribution.
        </p>
    </statement>
</definition>
 </page>
